## Initial Commit to branch postgres_setup
AKA, what I developed in the first week.

My first area of focus was learning how to run SQL files or run postgresQL terminal commands using bash scripts. The idea is to ensure that the database *structure* can be uniformly set up using code. AKA, since you can't really publish the postgres database itself to GitHub, you can at least publish code that creates an identical database.

I will probably switch to using docker for this purpose, but if that doesn't work, I will fall back on this approach. For the moment, it is pushed to branch postgres_setup.

Assuming postgres is installed locally, my database structure is achieved using the following bash scripts:

- role_setup.sh

- db_setup.sh

These can be run in a git bash terminal by simply writing:

bash [scriptname].sh

The first script requires a superuser to login, and create a new role whose only privileges is CREATEDB.

This script should be run first, since the intent is to have the rest of the codebase not relying on the superuser. All other scripts, including *db_setup.sh*, refers to the role created by running *role_setup.sh*.

As for *db_setup.sh*: Using the new role, it creates a database and a table based on the design specified in src/setup/table_setup.sql. THe idea is that this sql-file can be tailored to whatever the project needs, without needing to modify the bash scripts themselves.

Aside from that sql file, the bash scripts require a file called "src/config.py", in which sensitive variables that should not be pushed to github is specified. This includes the new role's username and password, as well as other relevant connectivity information like ports, etc. A user must write this config file themselves, and it is naturally included in .gitignore.

### Thoughts on this approach:

I have developed a script, db_reset.sh, which also requires superuser access, which undoes everything done by the other scripts. Mainly used for testing if the code works, so not uploaded to github just yet.

It does work. Running bash db_reset.sh deletes the role and database specified in src/config.py, allowing the scripts to be rerun.

I still think docker might be a more useful approach. Looking at that now.


